// A @none operator rejects the second "<" of `1 < 2 < 3` with an error action, and lookahead correction finds that error
// before the reductions which lead to it. Canonical LR(1) reduces here as well: "<" does follow an operand and a
// comparison, it is only the error action of the state after `comparison "<" comparison` which refuses it. So without
// lookahead correction the parser reduces the "2" to an operand and a comparison and reports the error in a state which
// expects only the end of input.
//
// The state after a number is not consistent, because it shifts "!" as well. Lookahead correction checks the "<" there,
// runs into the error action on the side, and reports the error with the number still on the stack. The message lists
// the "!" which would have been accepted. Compare nonassoc-error, where the state after a number is consistent and the
// reductions happen before the error.
//
// The scanner is not what this case is about, so its rules are single characters and runs of digits.

@scanner {
    WHITESPACE: /[ \t\n]+/ @skip;

    NUMBER: /[0-9]+/;
    LESS:   "<";
    BANG:   "!";
}

@parser {
    @precedence {
        @none: "<";
    }

    comparison
        : operand
        | comparison "<" comparison
        ;

    operand
        : NUMBER
        | NUMBER "!"
        ;
}
